From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hayaksi1 <193020925+hayaksi1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 20:44:59 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Teach the mention-naming case about m.mentions

Upstream started emitting an m.mentions block on every composed message
and updated its own expectations to match, but the fork case that pins
how a mention is named in the body predates that and still asserted the
old four-key shape, so an exact-equality check failed on a key the test
was never about. The sibling case one block up already carries the same
key, so this only brings the two into line.

The nested lookup that reads m.mentions back off m.new_content also had
no typing to stand on, since that inner value is a plain message content
which carries no index signature. Widening it through unknown at the one
call site keeps the assertion honest without loosening the exported type.
---
 .../utils/createMessageContent.test.ts               | 12 +++++++-----
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/apps/web/src/components/views/rooms/wysiwyg_composer/utils/createMessageContent.test.ts b/apps/web/src/components/views/rooms/wysiwyg_composer/utils/createMessageContent.test.ts
index b2c9027875..945d7bc545 100644
--- a/apps/web/src/components/views/rooms/wysiwyg_composer/utils/createMessageContent.test.ts
+++ b/apps/web/src/components/views/rooms/wysiwyg_composer/utils/createMessageContent.test.ts
@@ -50,10 +50,11 @@ describe("createMessageContent", () => {
 
             // Then
             expect(content).toEqual({
-                body: "Alice hello",
-                format: "org.matrix.custom.html",
-                formatted_body: mention,
-                msgtype: "m.text",
+                "body": "Alice hello",
+                "format": "org.matrix.custom.html",
+                "formatted_body": mention,
+                "msgtype": "m.text",
+                "m.mentions": {},
             });
         });
 
@@ -232,7 +233,8 @@ describe("createMessageContent", () => {
 
             // Bob was already notified by the message being edited, so only Carol is new.
             expect(content["m.mentions"]).toEqual({ user_ids: ["@carol:element.io"] });
-            expect(content["m.new_content"]!["m.mentions"]).toEqual({
+            const newContent = content["m.new_content"] as unknown as Record<string, unknown>;
+            expect(newContent["m.mentions"]).toEqual({
                 user_ids: ["@bob:element.io", "@carol:element.io"],
             });
         });
